cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use Duetkifu, please cite it as below."
title: "Duetkifu: a research record of every move, dead ends included, that humans and AI agents keep together"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Wu
    given-names: Shu Chi
version: 0.8.0
date-released: 2026-09-27
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/Ashur5457/duetkifu"
url: "https://github.com/Ashur5457/duetkifu"
abstract: >-
  Duetkifu keeps a research project as two plain JSON files that a researcher
  and an AI agent write together: a report for readers, and a research record
  (kifu, the record of a game of shogi or go) of every move of the research,
  including dead ends. Each move states the move it follows, why it was made,
  what came of it and, for a failure, why it ended, with a cause that tells a
  wrong idea from data that cannot be trusted. Each move also says where its
  numbers come from at one of three levels: recomputed from the raw files by
  recorded scripts (with checks of every stated number), sourced from named
  files with SHA-256 fingerprints, or no data with a reason. The record is
  drawn as an editable tree in a single-file HTML page; the report is reviewed
  in the same page with comments, free-hand regions on charts and a change
  history of every edit by human or agent. Agents answer comments on the
  report or on a single move, and search large folders through a local
  full-text index instead of reading whole files. Duetkifu builds on
  Duetsheet, whose report format it uses.
keywords:
  - research record
  - provenance
  - reproducibility
  - negative results
  - human-in-the-loop
  - AI agents
  - LLM
  - lab notebook
  - scientific workflow
  - annotation
